# Elements fixing Φ pointwise lie in ±Γ₁(M)

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.LevelN.Descent.fixer_le` (`533e00aa-e878-5a51-adb4-056c05d03b4d`),
by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `cb286391-1144-5745-8a41-7baf91995d6d`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix a positive integer $M$, a field $K$ equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure, an element
$t\in K$, and a group homomorphism $\sigma\colon \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})\to
\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb{C}}(K)$ whose kernel is the subgroup generated by $\Gamma(M)$ together with
$-1$; assume $K$ is finite-dimensional and Galois over the subfield $\mathbb{C}\langle t\rangle$
generated by $t$, of degree equal to the index of $\Gamma(M)\cdot\langle -1\rangle$ in
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$. Let $\iota\colon \overline{\mathbb{Q}}\to\mathbb{C}$ be a ring
homomorphism, and let $F$ denote the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ obtained by
adjoining to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ the coefficientwise images of the field generated over
$\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ by the quotients of integral $q$-expansions of pairs of
modular forms of equal weight on $\Gamma_1(M)$ (denominator nonzero). Let $\Phi\colon F\to K$ be a
ring homomorphism and $E'\colon K\to\mathbb{C}((q))$ a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphism with
$E'(t)$ the series $q^{-1}\cdot(E_4^3\eta^{-24}$-type integral expansion$)$ of $j$ with $q$ replaced
by $q^M$, and $E'(\Phi u)$ equal to the $q\mapsto q^M$ substitution applied to the $\iota$-image of
$u$ for every $u\in F$. Assume further that $\Phi$ sends the element $y_0\in F$ whose Laurent series
is the $j$-expansion over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to $t$, and that $\sigma(\gamma)$ fixes $\Phi u$
for all $\gamma\in\Gamma_1(M)$ and all $u$. Then any $\delta\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ with
$\sigma(\delta)(\Phi u)=\Phi u$ for all $u$ lies in the subgroup generated by $\Gamma_1(M)$ and
$-1$.

This is the Galois-correspondence step identifying the pointwise fixer of the image of the function
field of $X_1(M)$ inside $\mathrm{Gal}(K/\mathbb{C}(t))$ as exactly $\pm\Gamma_1(M)/\pm\Gamma(M)$,
the inclusion in the nontrivial direction. It is used in the descent computation of ramification
orders for the $\Gamma_1(M)$ base change over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, via the degree formula
$[\,\mathbb{C}\cdot F : \mathbb{C}(j(q^M))\,]=[\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}):\pm\Gamma_1(M)]$.

